1. Ontario Health Premium vs. Taxable Income Chart
> Software Used
This chart was created using Python with Matplotlib, offering programmatic control and reproducibility. It’s ideal for visualizing structured government datasets.
> Intended Audience
Ontario residents, healthcare economists, policy analysts, tax professionals, and students studying healthcare financing.
> Message Conveyed
The chart shows how the Ontario Health Premium increases with individual taxable income, revealing a progressive structure that levels off at higher incomes. It illustrates income thresholds where premiums rise in discrete steps before capping.
> Design Considerations
Axes: Clearly labeled in CAD for clarity.
Line + Dots: Depict discrete income points and inflection in premium changes.
Minimalist Layout: Avoids clutter to highlight trend transitions.
> Reproducibility
Highly reproducible through Python scripting. The logic, formulas, and data source (e.g., Ontario Ministry of Finance tables) can be shared and rerun.
> Accessibility
The simple layout and contrast support readability. To improve accessibility, alt-text, annotations, and a supporting data table should be included for screen readers.
> Impacted Communities
All income-tax-paying Ontarians, especially low- and middle-income individuals affected by sharp premium jumps.
> Feature Selection
Included: Taxable income and premium amounts.
Excluded: Family status or year-specific inflation adjustments for simplicity and focus.
> Underwater Labour
Sourcing and interpreting Ontario’s tax code
Scripting conditional logic for stepwise premiums
Styling with proper axis formatting and spacing